// ADR-0076 追記34 V1 guard: a program holding row-E closure VALUES
// compiles them under the __ev-taking convention, so a migration that
// cannot complete must be a HARD error — never a silent replay fallback
// (an unmigrated caller would call a migrated closure at the wrong
// arity). Ineligibility here comes from `via`, a ROW-VARIABLE callee in
// the needing wrapper's body (not pure, not needing — the pass cannot
// see through it). The wrapper is non-trivial (dtpw won't inline it) and
// the literal CAPTURES `base` (dlh won't hoist it), so no upstream
// normalization can legalize this shape — the earlier draft of this
// fixture used a nested same-effect handle and was silently normalized
// into a legitimately compilable program.
//
// #2065: `via` takes a FUNCTION-typed parameter, and that is load-bearing
// rather than incidental. `edp_callee_first_order` now admits a row-variable
// callee whose parameters (and return type) mention no function type, because
// then no argument can instantiate the row variable to the handled effect.
// A first-order `fn via(n: Int) -> Int with e` — which is what this fixture
// used to declare — is exactly that shape, so it would now be admitted, the
// migration would plan, and this fixture would compile and silently stop
// pinning anything. The function parameter is what keeps the row variable
// genuinely un-seeable, which is the condition the V1 guard exists for.
// The first-order case has its own pair:
// fixtures/async_first_order_rowvar_boundary_test.vibe (admitted) and
// fixtures/err_async_rowvar_higher_order_refused.vibe (still refused).
//
// Since the call-site argument-inertness rule (#2065, queries.vibe
// `edp_argcond_admits`), a CLOSURE LITERAL that cannot perform the effect in
// that position is admitted too -- the literal `(y) -> y` this fixture used
// to pass now compiles and answers correctly (pinned by
// fixtures/evidence_rowvar_literal_arg_test.vibe). The argument is a NAMED
// function instead: its row is not readable at the call site, so the row
// variable stays genuinely un-seeable.
fn ident(y: Int) -> Int {
  y
}

effect Ask {
  Get -> Int
}

fn via(n: Int, g: (Int) -> Int) -> Int with e {
  g(n)
}

let apply2 = (f: () -> Int with Ask) -> Int with Ask {
  let x = f()
  via(x, ident)
}

let _start = () -> Int {
  let base = 7
  let lit = () -> Int with Ask {
    perform Ask::Get + base
  }
  handle {
    apply2(lit)
  } with {
    Ask::Get => resume(1)
  }
}
